From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:51:14 -0400
Subject: routing: a site opens in the Space it is routed to, with a toast that
 undoes it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

spaces::SpaceRouter is a pure function over the profile pref
stedding.spaces.routes (site, contains or equals rules, first match wins)
plus stedding.spaces.external_default, the Space for links from other
apps. A tab inserted with a routed address, or a fresh tab given a typed
address, moves to its Space at once and the window follows a foreground
open a turn later; a tab rebuilt from a session is never routed. Links
handed over by another app are marked for a moment so they can land in
the default Space. A toast "Opened in <Space> · Undo" puts the tab back.
Rules are made in place: the tab menu's "Always Open <site> in" lists the
Spaces and writes a site rule, and chrome://settings/stedding shows each
Space's rules under its row with a remove control and an add field, plus
the dropdown for links from other apps. Peek runs first, so a link that
peeks is never routed (docs/features/routing.md D1-D6).

Why: docs/ROUND6-PLAN.md wave 3, R6-23: Arc's Air Traffic Control.
Removable when: Chromium routes new tabs to workspaces by site.
